WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.730
What happened to Charlie from Willy Wonka?

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.000
What about Newt from Aliens?

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.029
And which child sitcom actor teaches people
to pilot submarines?

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:09.070
All that and more ahead!

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:14.400
James Cameron's "Aliens" went in a decidedly
different direction than its Ridley Scott-directed

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.400
predecessor.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.369
While "Alien" was essentially a haunted house
movie in space, the sequel became an action-packed

00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:21.119
military adventure film.

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:24.599
Instead of a single xenomorph feeding on the
crew of one ship, dozens of the creatures

00:00:24.599 --> 00:00:28.949
battle Ellen Ripley, played by Sigourney Weaver,
and the team of space Marines sent to wipe

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:29.949
them out.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:33.360
The director's cut of the Cameron film also
added depth to the Ripley character by letting

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.440
the audience know that she lost her daughter
while in hypersleep.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:40.180
So when she comes across a lost little girl
named Rebecca Jorden that everyone calls Newt,

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.840
her maternal instincts kick in and her mission
becomes to keep this girl alive.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.510
Newt was portrayed by Carrie Henn, who was
only 10 at the time.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.940
Following the success of the film, one would
imagine she'd want to build on that momentum.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.870
Instead, she decided to let herself be a normal
kid.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:57.900
While talking to AVP Galaxy, she explained
that she'd been living in England until the

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.580
military transferred her father back to the
United States.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:04.689
Now she was the odd person out and wanted
to be as "normal" as possible, so she left

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:05.909
acting behind.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:09.390
As an adult, Henn decided to become a teacher,
satisfying a childhood dream.

00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:14.909
The family sitcom "Home Improvement," starring
Tim Allen, ran for eight seasons from 1991

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:16.310
to 1999.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:20.389
The show chronicled the life of delusional
television personality Tim "The Tool Man"

00:01:20.389 --> 00:01:21.920
Taylor trying to relate to his family.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.880
The youngest of those sons, Mark, is more
sensitive and introverted than the rest of

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:26.880
his family.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.189
He was played by young actor Taran Noah Smith.

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:32.220
Following the end of the series, Smith decided
to leave acting behind.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.740
When he was 17, the actor had a public dispute
with his parents over the handling of the

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.409
money he earned during the production of "Home
Improvement."

00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:43.429
While talking with HNGN, Smith said he enjoyed
acting but after doing so for his entire childhood,

00:01:43.429 --> 00:01:45.080
he wanted to try something else.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.889
As mentioned in the interview, Smith has helped
with typhoon relief efforts in the Philippines

00:01:48.889 --> 00:01:50.200
and with various art installations.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.689
He and his former wife also started a vegan
food and restaurant business, called Playfood,

00:01:54.689 --> 00:01:55.959
in 2005.

00:01:55.959 --> 00:01:59.810
Another one of his jobs, however, is teaching
people how to pilot submarines, which he had

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.689
been doing as late as 2019.

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:07.179
Ross Bagley started acting at the age of six
when he appeared as Buckwheat in the 1994

00:02:07.179 --> 00:02:09.140
big screen reboot of "The Little Rascals."

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.550
In 1996, he joined the cast of the Will Smith
sitcom "The Fresh Prince of Bel Air," where

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:15.260
he played Will's youngest cousin Nicky.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.780
That same year he worked with Smith again
in the blockbuster "Independence Day."

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:22.920
While his IMDb page shows that he continued
to pop up in small roles for the rest of the

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.020
'90s, Bagley seems to have left acting behind.

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.910
During an appearance on the "Hip Hop Uncensored
Podcast," Bagley discussed his time on "The

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:32.610
Fresh Prince" and his life following the end
of the series.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.080
Before getting cast as Nicky, he loved watching
the show.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:39.030
He has fond memories of working on it, but
now prefers to live a more low-key lifestyle

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:40.410
out of the public eye.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.920
Instead of pursuing the constant grind that
is a show business career, Bagley has a more

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.180
steady job as a realtor.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.720
Margaret Langrick started acting when she
was 14.

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.090
Her first film role was Sandy Wilcox in the
1985 film "My American Cousin."

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.930
She followed that up with the Bigfoot comedy
"Harry and the Hendersons."

00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:00.220
IMDb lists her final acting credit as "Sweet
Angel Mine" in 1996.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:04.040
Since then, Langrick has gone on to have an
impressive and prolific career in the world

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.040
of publishing.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.710
According to her website, the former actress
spent 15 years working as an editor in the

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:09.850
UK and Canada.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:13.370
This work included serving as the Arts and
Life editor for the Vancouver Sun.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:17.180
After years of making people happy with her
film and television roles, Langrick continues

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.870
to motivate people through her publishing.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:24.120
In 2013, she founded LifeTree Media, which
is now called Wonderwell, a hybrid non-fiction

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.720
publishing house specializing in books that,
quote, "help, heal and inspire."

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:32.190
Jeff Cohen was all over the place during the
early years of the '80s.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:36.220
Starting in 1983, the young actor was all
over our TV screens, though he’s mostly

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:37.930
remembered from the film "The Goonies."

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:42.390
He played Chunk, the hilarious kid with a
penchant for telling tall tales and clumsiness.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.780
His performance in that film was so confident
and assured that the fact that he is no longer

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.640
performing is mind-boggling.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.040
But it turns out that the former actor has
a theory as to why his career ended after

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:52.430
only eight years.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:55.940
While talking to the Daily Mail, Cohen clearly
stated that he wasn't the one who gave up

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:56.940
on acting.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:58.740
He started his career as the funny overweight
kid.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:02.640
That was how the business saw him, so when
he started hitting puberty and losing the

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.160
weight, they weren't quite sure what to do
with him.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:06.160
He said,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:07.160
"It was terrible.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.960
My first love was acting but puberty had other
ideas.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:09.960
It was a forced retirement.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.960
I didn't give up on acting.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.069
Acting gave up on me."

00:04:13.069 --> 00:04:16.220
With the help of his friend, "The Goonies"
director Richard Donner, Cohen learned more

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.720
about the film business and studied law at
UCLA.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.160
In 2002, he co-founded his law firm Cohen
Gardner LLP, which is based in Beverly Hills

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.720
and specializes in the many legal aspects
of film and TV development, production and

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:29.720
distribution.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.470
Josh Saviano was 12 when he started acting.

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:35.840
His very first role was as Kid Belz in "The
Wrong Guys" from 1988.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.630
That same year he was cast as Paul Pfeiffer
in the coming-of-age TV classic "The Wonder

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:41.630
Years."

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:44.930
During that show's five-year run, audiences
watched as both Kevin Arnold, played by Fred

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:49.340
Savage, and his best friend Paul navigated
the turbulence of the 1960s and their teenage

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:50.340
years.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:53.639
While most of his co-stars continued their
careers in show business, Saviano left acting

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:54.639
behind.

00:04:54.639 --> 00:04:58.080
Aside from a few guest appearances on "Law
and Order: Special Victims Unit," and being

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.040
confused for Marilyn Manson, Saviano has stayed
out of the spotlight.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:04.740
When "The Wonder Years" ended, Saviano attended
Yale Law school.

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:08.110
According to his LinkedIn page, he is now
putting his knowledge of show business to

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:12.190
use by helping artists make the most out of
their careers through his own firm, Spotlight

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:13.190
Advisory Group.

00:05:13.190 --> 00:05:14.190
He told Yahoo,

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:17.590
"What I'm trying to do these days is help
individual artists and talent brand themselves

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:21.610
— whether it is through a reality television
show, or through just being a social media

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.310
meme, or other skill set that they have."

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.810
Lisa Jakub started working in show business
when she was seven and worked like crazy from

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:29.980
1985 to 2000.

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:33.889
The young actress starred in all kinds of
productions before landing her most memorable

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:37.400
role in the 1993 Robin Williams comedy "Mrs.
Doubtfire."

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:41.340
She played Lydia in the film about a divorced
dad who dresses up as a matronly Scottish

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.190
woman to spend more time with his kids.

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:46.270
That wasn't her only big movie of the '90s
either.

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:50.090
In 1996, she appeared in the alien invasion
hit "Independence Day."

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:52.560
Despite these successes, however, she decided
to leave acting.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.150
On her blog, she discussed her reasons.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:59.040
After all that time, the pressure and competitiveness
of the job got to be too much.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.040
She wrote,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.470
"I decided I should leave, before I became
one of those alcoholic/eating disorder ravaged/drug

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.800
addicted train wrecks of a former child actor.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.020
I had no desire to be a cautionary tale."

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.810
The experience had taken such a toll on her
that she refused for a long time to engage

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:14.740
in discussions about her early life.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:17.950
Instead, she focused on being a normal person
out of the spotlight.

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:21.260
But she's since made peace with that time
of her life and has even written extensively

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:22.260
about it.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:23.400
Her other passion is yoga instruction.

00:06:24.470 --> 00:06:28.560
"All That" was Nickelodeon's 1990s answer
to "Saturday Night Live."

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:32.460
Like the sketch comedy juggernaut, it helped
launch impressive careers for its stars.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:36.940
Perhaps one of the more fascinating post-Nickelodeon
careers is that of Lori Beth Denberg.

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:40.750
The actress was best known on "All That" for
the Loud Librarian, who shushed anyone who

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:44.920
made even the slightest noise, while she went
on yelling and making a racket.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.310
“No talking!

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:47.930
This is a libary!

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:48.930
It must be quiet!”

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:52.840
Her other recurring segment was "Vital Information
for Your Everyday Life," where she would often

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.830
give viewers terrible advice.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:58.400
As discussed during an interview with Vice,
Denberg decided to try other things as she

00:06:58.400 --> 00:06:59.400
got older.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.699
“My work here is done.

00:07:01.699 --> 00:07:02.699
Goodbye everyone.”

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:06.800
Aside from the touring she does with fellow
"All That" alum Danny Tamberelli, she also

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.270
officiates weddings.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:12.030
In the "about" section of her website, she
explains the kinds of wedding experiences

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.300
she has, writing,

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:17.090
“Personalized, quirky weddings, vow renewals,
and commitment ceremonies for couples looking

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:20.490
for something a little different, a little
less sterile, and a little more fun."

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:27.260
From "Beverly Hills 90210" in 1990 to "Without
A Trace" in 2006, Ross Malinger worked as

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:28.630
an actor on a regular basis.

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:33.580
He is likely best remembered for roles in
"Kindergarten Cop" and the 1993 romantic comedy

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:34.669
"Sleepless in Seattle."

00:07:34.669 --> 00:07:38.860
In "Sleepless in Seattle," Malinger played
Jonah Baldwin, the son of Tom Hanks' character,

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:39.860
Sam Baldwin.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:44.490
Sam is a widower who relocated from Chicago
to Seattle and shows no visible signs of interest

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:45.620
in finding love.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:49.540
His son calls into a radio show and asks his
father to discuss his feelings following the

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:50.800
loss of his wife.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:53.960
Jonah is essentially responsible for the entire
plot of the film.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:57.800
Malinger's ability as an actor is obvious
from the very beginning and clearly served

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:58.900
him well for years.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:03.460
Still, after one last guest shot in 2006 on
"Without A Trace," the actor left his profession

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:04.460
behind.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:08.280
According to the Hollywood Reporter, he then
went into automotive sales, working as a manager

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:12.520
at one dealership until it went out of business
in 2009 and finding work at others around

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:13.949
Los Angeles since then.

00:08:14.949 --> 00:08:18.540
Released in 1971, "Willy Wonka and the Chocolate
Factory" is a classic.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:22.759
The film tells the story of an eccentric candymaker
named Willy Wonka who opens his whimsical

00:08:22.759 --> 00:08:24.740
factory to the public for the first time.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:29.040
To be allowed into his bizarre domain of lickable
wallpaper, chocolate rivers, and Oompa Loompas,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.149
you have to find one of the golden tickets
he scattered in Wonka Bars across the globe.

00:08:33.149 --> 00:08:37.110
A desperately poor kid with a big heart named
Charlie Bucket is one of the lucky few who

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:38.349
discovers a ticket.

00:08:38.349 --> 00:08:42.370
The film is about how his kindness makes all
his dreams come true when Wonka realizes he

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:44.470
is the person who should inherit his factory.

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:48.850
Charlie was played by a young Peter Ostrum
and is his only credited role as an actor.

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:52.800
These days, instead of working with chocolate,
he works as a dairy veterinarian with Countryside

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:54.790
Veterinary Clinic in Lowville, New York.

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:58.890
While speaking with the Journal of the American
Veterinary Medical Association, Dr. Ostrum

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:02.710
explained that after working on "Willy Wonka"
he fell in love with taking care of animals.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:03.710
He said,

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:07.000
"Acting was fine, but I wanted something more
steady, and the key is to find something that

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:09.810
you love doing, and that's what my profession
has given to me."

00:09:11.060 --> 00:09:13.740
Julianna Rose Mauriello started her acting
career at the age of six when she appeared

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:16.520
in "Sesame Street: Fiesta!" in 1996.

00:09:16.520 --> 00:09:20.230
She went on to do the voice of Ruby in the
animated series about bunnies, "Max and Ruby,"

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:21.230
in 2002.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:25.240
Perhaps her best-known role, however, is that
of Stephanie on the hit Nickelodeon series

00:09:25.240 --> 00:09:26.240
"LazyTown."

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:29.860
Why did she stop acting and what has she been
doing since 2013?

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:33.670
Based on the lack of details from official
sources, any statement on her reason for leaving

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:35.610
show business would be speculation.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:39.320
What is clear, however, is that Mauriello
is now working as a pediatric occupational

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:40.320
therapist.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.870
According to her LinkedIn profile, she attended
Columbia University Vagelos College of Physicians

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:47.890
and Surgeons and is practicing in a clinic
environment.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:51.630
Whatever her reasons for leaving her old career,
one would hope she is happy and fulfilled

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:52.700
with her new one.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:54.580
“I think this year’s gonna be different!”

